{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And you shall say, 'Moreover, your servant Jacob is behind us.'</b> There is a great question here. Since he said to the second and third drove, 'According to this word shall you speak' — this implies that whatever the first ones spoke, you too shall speak. If so, why did he need to single out from the general rule and specify 'And you shall say, Moreover, your servant Jacob is behind us'? For this was already stated for the first drove, that they should say thus, and when he said to the second and third drove, 'According to this word shall you speak,' etc., he surely included in this what the first drove said — and this too is included. Why then did he repeat to say to the second drove, 'And you shall say, Moreover, behold' etc.?" "<b>Answer to the matter.</b> For Jacob said: When Esau my brother meets you and asks you three questions — 'To whom do you belong?' 'Where are you going?' 'To whom are these before you?' — you shall answer him one by one. To whom do you belong? 'And you shall say, To your servant, to Jacob.' Where are you going? To deliver the gift, for 'It is a gift sent.' To whom are these before you? To whom is it sent? 'To my lord, to Esau.' And beyond these three questions, even if he does not ask you 'Where is Jacob?' — nevertheless you shall say to him of your own accord, 'Moreover, behold, he is behind us,' so that it should not be evil in his eyes that I am honoring him by means of sent emissaries and I am not going myself to meet him face to face. And when Jacob said to the second and to the third drove, 'According to this word shall you speak,' it is obvious that they need to answer his three questions — for from the standpoint that if one of these three is not answered, the entire mission of that drove is voided: for if it does not come from Jacob, or it does not go to Esau, or it comes and goes but this gift is not for Esau — if so, with what will this drove propitiate his face? But the statement 'And behold, he too is behind us' is not an answer to his questions, nor is acceptance of the gift so dependent on this; for whether he comes or does not come, after all the gift is sent. This is nothing but the telling of a matter in addition to his questions. You might have thought: since the first drove already reported that Jacob would come after us, there is no need for the second drove to report as well that he too is behind us — for this report is superfluous. Therefore Jacob needed to specify that the second drove too should say, 'And behold, he too is behind us.' The reason for this: he feared lest the first drove appear in Esau's eyes as liars, for when they say 'And behold, he too is behind us,' he will understand from their words that immediately, at once, right after that drove, Jacob will come. And when he sees that he does not come with the second drove, he will say that the first drove lied in saying 'And behold, he too is behind us.' Therefore he commanded that the second drove too should say, 'And behold, he too is behind us,' and for this very same reason he also commanded the third drove to say so."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:44.141172708Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}